❌ Critical Error 1: The Repository Mismatch
Your Code:
public interface OrderRepo extends JpaRepository<FoodItem, Long> { ... }

The Problem:
You created a Repository for the Child (FoodItem), but in your Service, you are trying to save the Parent (FoodOrder).
orderRepo.save(foodOrder) will fail because the repo expects a FoodItem.
The Fix:
Change the generic type to the Parent.
public interface OrderRepo extends JpaRepository<FoodOrder, Long> { ... }

❌ Critical Error 2: The "Broken Calculator" Loop
Your Code:
for(int i=0; i<orderRequest.getItems().size(); i++) {
     // 🚨 getFirst() always gets index 0! 
     // If you have 3 items, this adds the price of the 1st item 3 times.
     total += orderRequest.getItems().getFirst().getPrice();
}

The Fix (The Enhanced For-Loop):
In professional Java, we rarely use int i = 0. We use the "For-Each" loop. It reads like English: "For each Item inside the List..."
for (FoodItem item : orderRequest.getItems()) {
    total = total + item.getPrice();
}

❌ Critical Error 3: The Missing Connection
The Problem:
You calculated the total, but you forgot to put the list of items into the order object!
If you save this, the Database will have an Order, but the items will be gone.
The Fix:
foodOrder.setItems(orderRequest.getItems()); // Don't forget this line!

✅ The 100% Fixed Code
Here is the corrected Service. I switched to the "Enhanced For Loop" because it is much easier to read.
1. The Repository (Fixed)
@Repository
// ✅ Changed FoodItem -> FoodOrder. We save the Parent!
public interface OrderRepo extends JpaRepository<FoodOrder, Long> {
}

2. The Service (Fixed Logic)
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepo orderRepo;

    public OrderResponce createOrder(OrderRequest orderRequest) {

        FoodOrder foodOrder = new FoodOrder();
        foodOrder.setCustomerName(orderRequest.getCustomerName());
        foodOrder.setDeliveryAddress(orderRequest.getDeliveryAddress());

        // --- STEP 1: The Calculation Loop ---
        double total = 0;
        
        // "For every 'item' inside the 'items' list..."
        for (FoodItem item : orderRequest.getItems()) {
            total = total + item.getPrice(); // Add price to total
        }
        
        foodOrder.setTotalAmount(total);

        // --- STEP 2: The Missing Link ---
        // You MUST attach the list to the parent, or they won't save!
        foodOrder.setItems(orderRequest.getItems());

        // --- STEP 3: Save (Cascade saves everything) ---
        FoodOrder savedData = orderRepo.save(foodOrder);

        // --- STEP 4: Response ---
        OrderResponce response = new OrderResponce();
        response.setOrderId(savedData.getId());
        response.setCustomerName(savedData.getCustomerName());
        response.setTotalAmount(savedData.getTotalAmount());
        response.setStatus("PREPARING");

        return response;
    }
}

🧠 The "For Loop" Lesson (Since you said you struggle with Lists)
Imagine you are at a checkout counter with a cart of items.
Old Way (int i=0):
> "Pick up item at index 0. Add price. Put it down."
> "Pick up item at index 1. Add price. Put it down."
> This is prone to errors (like using getFirst() by mistake).
> 
New Way (for (Item item : list)):
> "Take the next item in the cart. Add price. Repeat until empty."
> This is impossible to mess up.
> 
